{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Let Reuben live.</b> He began with the firstborn, for so is the order, and he did not mention Simeon because of Baal Peor, for those who served were Simeonites, and their number was forever lost, and their prince was killed. Or it follows the way of Jacob, who did not bless Simeon and Levi, and because of Aaron's honor, the tribe was suspended from him [Simeon], and there was none in the tribe of Simeon as great as he. And if one were to argue: why did he bless Reuben? The answer is that Jacob blessed him, and the blessing is 'let him not excel, that he be as one of his brothers.' And after Reuben he mentioned Judah, for he is a banner bearer, and he goes up first to war, and then Levi, and after him Benjamin, because the Levites dwelt in Jerusalem, which was between Judah and Benjamin, so it is written. And then the tribe of Joseph, so as not to delay him, and he had already preceded him — his younger brother. And then Zebulun, because he fights, and then Issachar. And when the sons of the handmaids were finished, he began with Gad, for he is with the banner of the sons of the handmaids, and then Dan, for he is a banner bearer, and then Naphtali, for he is greater than Asher." "<b>Let Reuben live.</b> A prayer." "<b>And let him not die</b> — as though it were possible for him to be like a human being, to live a fixed number of years and die." "<b>And let his men be a number.</b> Let his men not be a number, like 'and I have not learned wisdom' [with a negative]. [This is consistent] with El Shaddai [i.e., the bet of באל] as I interpreted for many, for it is possible that he live forever, and his men be few, and anything that is counted is few. And likewise, 'And I am as one who is dead' [מתי מספר]."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:26:26.464761121Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}